 Carter stood at the window with a memory vial and thought of green things growing."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Remembering', 'text': "By dawn the Patrol had stood down — human, after all, and shown the same thirty seconds. Director Vane was gone by noon, the Algorithm's servers dark. the Sprawl belonged to its people again. It had only ever been on loan.\n\nWhat happened in the next hour, historians argue about. What Nyx Carter remembers: silence, then a sound like a held breath released, then the streets filling with people who had remembered they were people.\n\nNyx Carter and the runner Pax were in the tower when the door blew. The ex-enforcer held the stairs long enough. Long enough for what? For the footage to loop. For the memory to root. For Director Vane's lie to die on every screen at once."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'After the Static', 'text': "The footage from a memory vial became the Sprawl's founding document: not a constitution, but a memory. This is what we were. This is what we can be. the system fears the truth — carved, at the runner Pax's insistence, over the tower door.\n\nAnd when the new council asked Nyx Carter to lead, Nyx Carter laughed and went back to the food lines. Leaders, Nyx Carter had learned, are just people who show up. Nyx Carter shows up.\n\nRebuilding the Sprawl was harder than freeing it. Nyx Carter organized food lines with the same care once used for heists. the runner Pax taught kids to code on salvaged terminals. The medic ran a clinic. The runner ran messages of hope."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Habit of Freedom', 'text': "the system fears the truth — Nyx Carter thinks it every time, like a prayer, like a promise. Memory is resistance. Freedom is a habit. Practice daily.\n\nAnd somewhere, in a gutter where drones don't look, someone is finding something warm that shouldn't be warm. It starts again. It always starts again. That's the best part.\n\nthe runner Pax runs the network now — the real one, the open one, the one that belongs